===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Greek mythology}} The [[legendary]] [[knot]] tied to a pole near the temple of [[Zeus]] in [[Gordium]].
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any [[intricate]] and [[complex]] [[problem]] having a simple solution.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|i|1|text=List his discourse of war, and you shall hear<br/>A fearful battle rend'red you in music;<br/>Turn him to any cause of policy,<br/>The '''Gordian knot''' of it he will unloose.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=August 19 1785|author=w:Thomas Jefferson|title=[[s:Letter to Peter Carr - August 19, 1785|Letter to Peter Carr]]
|passage=The '''knot''' which you thought a '''Gordian''' one, will untie itself before you.}}
#* {{RQ:Hazlitt Spirit of the Age|chapter=The Late Mr. Horne Tooke|passage=Mr. Tooke thought he had answered this question satisfactorily, and loosened the '''Gordian knot''' of grammarians{{...|, “familiar as his garter,” when he said, “It is the common pronoun, adjective, or participle, that, with the noun, thing or proposition, implied, and the particular example following it.”}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=w:James Greenwood|title=s:Low-Life Deeps|chapter=10
|passage=The right hon. gentleman, who was doubtless aware of the popularity of the old English saying, "what is sauce for the goose should be sauce for the gander," shrewdly judged that he could not go far wrong if he cut the '''Gordian knot''' in which the publican's disagreement was bound, by declaring his conviction that what was good for the Crown and Cushion, in the Strand, was likewise good for the Three Jolly Tinkers, in Brick Lane; and that, to put an end to the vexed question, the shortest way would be to tar them all with the same brush.}}

